WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.900
- Doctors are notoriously bad patients.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.550
And I fall into that category, headstrong.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.440
And recently, I went for my checkup

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.840
and found out something
really bad about my health.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.060
And I want to share that with you.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.480
Huge, thanks to Walmart Plus
for sponsoring this video.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.660
Peewoop.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.810
I've been kind of slacking on my health

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.370
over the last year and a half.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:20.870
I'd like to blame the pandemic.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.210
And that's certainly been a part of it,

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.610
but a lot of it has also come from

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.500
the fact that I've been a little bit lazy

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.500
and I've been slacking

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.400
and that's okay.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.980
But when push comes to shove,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.630
we have to take care of our health.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.630
But I knew, for me

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.460
it was time to get a checkup,
get my blood work done,

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.570
figure out what my risk factors were.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.640
And this is a really
important teaching point here

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.940
because you don't need to get
blood work done all the time.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.350
It's about knowing the right intervals

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.340
of when to get your blood check.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.770
That's why it's so important to have

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.670
a family medicine doctor to guide you on

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.160
when you should be getting
your cholesterol checked,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.780
when you should get your sugar checked.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.090
Blood work has to be
ordered with a purpose

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.930
because we treat the
patient, not the lab value.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.130
In fact, when you get
blood work done too often,

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.210
that increases the rate of medical error

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.630
cause something could be off
just in that given moment.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.800
And it really confuses
and muddles the picture.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.470
Not to mention the healthcare spending

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:09.303
that comes with that.

00:01:09.303 --> 00:01:10.970
For me, one of the
tests I needed to order,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.380
especially knowing how poor

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.730
my diet has been over the last year.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.340
I needed to check my cholesterol.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.254
I didn't expect this at all.

00:01:18.254 --> 00:01:20.000
My cholesterol was through the roof.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.170
In fact, it was getting
to be at such a high level

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.917
that it was almost falling
into the category of

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:27.610
"You can't get it this
high with just the diet.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.370
We need to check if you
have a familial gene

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.990
for hypercholesterolemia".

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:34.940
If I had a patient my age
walked in with that cholesterol,

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.730
I would have a very serious conversation

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:37.890
about genetic testing.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.580
My number, my LDL cholesterol was 185.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.720
Getting into the 190's is like,

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.730
potentially have a heart attack at age 40

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.280
with no symptoms, bad.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.310
And I don't want to have a heart attack.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.940
I don't want to die.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.810
It was a brutal wake-up call for me.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.250
The general goal is we want to get

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.010
the LDL cholesterol below a hundred.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.090
So in going back to see what I was doing,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.550
I realized I developed
some really bad habits

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.400
and they're bad habits

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.900
that I want to highlight with you today

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.970
because it goes to argue the point

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.120
that weight is not the single determinant

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.770
of your health status.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.900
I was not eating a lot of
calories during the day.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.010
Therefore my weight was okay,

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.400
but my cholesterol markers were terrible

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.600
because of the type of
foods I was consuming.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.810
Here's how a typical day went.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.610
I would wake up, not eat anything.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.130
I'm a big fan of intermittent fasting.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:26.720
Don't have much of an
appetite in the morning.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.240
So then I would continue along
with my day till lunchtime,

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.870
where I would have a salad.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.360
And then came dinner time,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.980
I was still working cause
I work really late hours

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.300
where it would be like 10, 11, 12 am.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.370
And I'm done with work, but I'm starving.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:41.620
And I worked out in the morning.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.610
So I burned like 800 calories.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.790
I need to consume some
serious calories right now

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.640
in order to not lose weight
and keep some muscle on.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.560
So what I ended up doing was
binge eating late at night,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.090
really low quality foods

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.570
and extra cheese pizza
with pepperoni on it.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:55.790
I would take it to the face.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.670
A cheeseburger, double
cheeseburger, nuggets,

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.400
or some kind of like crispy shrimp.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.640
So for the day I only consume
like 22, 2300 calories

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.940
burned 800 in the morning.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.440
I was good. My body was looking great.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.270
Like my six-pack started even coming in,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.360
but my cholesterol levels

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.010
were quietly going through the roof.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.010
Because I was consuming
a lot of saturated fat,

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.900
a lot of fried foods, unhealthy foods,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.860
a lot of red meat, giant steak,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.170
like it was way too much red meat.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.260
Because I need someone to be objective,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.500
I reached out to my good
friend, Dr. Danielle Belardo,

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.480
who's a well-trained cardiologists,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.680
knows everything about
cholesterol, lowering cholesterol,

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.537
and I knew she could be of help.

00:03:31.537 --> 00:03:34.070
- [Dr. Danielle Belardo] LDL
Cholesterol is the actual,

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.730
most important cholesterol that we look at

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:39.190
with regards to cardiac risk.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.220
Even though I think you may have possibly

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.330
some genetic components
of high cholesterol,

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.430
I do think that there's a lot
you can do to reduce your LDL

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.330
with diet significantly.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.520
- Dr. Belardo gave me three tips

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.730
on how to lower my LDL cholesterol

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.140
from least to most
important that I follow.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.772
Third is to decrease dietary cholesterol.

00:03:57.772 --> 00:04:00.433
- [Dr.Danielle Belardo] Reducing
the amount of any sort of,

00:04:00.433 --> 00:04:02.915
kind of processed foods, junk foods,

00:04:02.915 --> 00:04:06.240
refined carbohydrates, that
can make a huge difference.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.160
- Second, is to bump up my fiber intake

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.567
to about 60 grams of fiber
per day. Not an easy task.

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:15.050
- [Dr. Danielle Belardo]
Fiber only exists in plants.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.380
And so by eating more plant-based foods,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.790
whether it be fruits,
vegetables, whole grains, legumes

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:25.810
this can help reduce
your cholesterol as well.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.864
And your LDL specifically.

00:04:27.864 --> 00:04:29.640
- And first and most important is

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.477
to decrease my saturated fat consumption.

00:04:32.477 --> 00:04:34.517
- [Dr. Danielle Belardo] Saturated fat

00:04:34.517 --> 00:04:36.090
we can see in both animal
products and plant products.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.270
For example, coconut oil is
really high in saturated fat.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.280
It can raise your LDL.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.070
So dropping a saturated
fat by cutting back

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.530
on red meat, butter, bacon,
cheese, or high-fat dairy,

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.100
that can make a huge difference too.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:50.690
- Knowing my cholesterol was so high,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.560
I knew I needed to start eating healthier.

